{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Boot.Dev for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Recent findings have brought us closer than \nwe’ve ever been to confirming life beyond the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth. As we wait for this to be confirmed \nor otherwise, let’s think about what this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14880,
    "dDurationMs": 12240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will tell us about life everywhere, and \nalso about our own imminent destruction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone—before two quick announcements \nbefore start, we need your help. The algorithm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is hiding episodes from subscribers. From past \nexperiments, we know the best way to help is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to like the episode AND leave a comment. That \ntells the algorithm to share it with more of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our community. And if you haven’t yet, subscribe \nand hit the bell so you don’t miss new episodes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50160,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re also excited to let you know \nabout the launch our first backpack:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54640,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Black Hole Bag! It holds a massive \n28 liters, with a padded laptop sleeve,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple compartments for easy organization, and \na roll-top design. Made from recycled polyester,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s durable, sustainable, and—like a black \nhole—nothing escapes. This premium item is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73680,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10% off for the first 48 hours. Link in \nthe description. Now, on to the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Three and a half billion years ago, two warm \nand watery planets twirled about a young G-type  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star. On both of their surfaces, chemical soups \nbubbled and complexified and organized themselves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93440,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into tiny lipid balls that learned harvest \nenergy, to duplicate themselves, to evolve.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their methods were endlessly creative. On the \nsmaller world, one lifeform learned to pluck a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107440,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spark of energy by reducing one iron compound into \na slightly more stable one. They’d take hold in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115040,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mineral-rich spot and spread outwards, a metabolic \nshockwave carving rings of vivianite into a damp  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122720,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "river delta. Who knows what else these clever \nlittle things might have become given time. How  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130080,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much cleverer. But they didn’t have time. Their \nworld was small. Its molten core cooled quickly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hardened, stopped spinning. Its magnetic field \nswitched off, and the delicate new biosphere  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143440,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was laid bare to the abrasive solar wind. \nWith the atmosphere lost, the world froze."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150720,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its larger neighbor fared better for a while. \nIt held its heat and so also its magnetism and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its atmosphere and its water and its carbon \ncycle. Life thrived, continued to complexify,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and eventually spawned some even cleverer little \ncreatures whose metabolic shockwave was much,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169600,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much larger. They learned to travel between the \nworlds, or at least to send their robots. And so,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3.5 billion years later after some \nrings were carved in a river delta,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183040,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the artifacts of two interplanetary neighbors met. \nThe robot of one touched the fossil of the other.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191200,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that moment, the remaining lifeform \nunderstood two things: that, even now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197920,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was far from alone in this universe, and \nthat its doom was closer than it imagined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In case you didn’t guess, the small world \nis Mars and the large one Earth. The robot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the Perseverance rover and the spots were \nthe subject of a recent NASA announcement as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a possible biosignature. You may have \nheard the news. But this isn’t a news  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "piece. Here we’re asking the question: what \ndoes the potential detection of ancient life  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on Mars or anywhere really mean for \nus? Why might it point to our doom?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234080,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll say more about the finding in a minute. \nFirst, let me give you an alternate version  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240800,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this story that’s at least equally likely. \nLife only formed … only COULD have formed in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the larger world. Abiogenesis—the formation \nof life from non-life—requires such specific  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "circumstances that it’ll never happen twice \nin the one solar system, and may happen very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260000,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rarely in an entire galaxy. Now the robots still \nexplored, but every pattern found in the rocks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every odd atmospheric composition, turned out \nto be explainable by non-living processes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274240,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They were phantoms of our fear of the truth—that \nwe’re relatively alone in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t know which of these stories are \ntrue—did Perseverance just discover ancient  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287360,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life on our literal neighbor, meaning that \nlife is extremely abundant in the universe?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294240,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or will we never discover life because it’s \ninsanely rare. It’s a hell of a thing to not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know which of these wildly different scenarios \nis true. That’s why the recent discovery by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA is so exciting. We’re on the precipice \nof possibly knowing one way or the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310640,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at the science before we peer deeper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313200,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the existential abyss of \nwhat this means either way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316560,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Perseverance rover landed in the Jezero \ncrater on February 18th, 2021. The ancient  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impact site was chosen to optimize the chance of \nfinding evidence of past life. A critical factor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is evidence of past water. It’s pretty clear \nthat Jezero was once a lake, with dry river beds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both entering and exiting. After meandering the \ncrater basin, Perseverance found its way to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neretva Vallis region, part of the ancient river \ndelta feeding the lake. Its sights were set on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Bright Angel—an unusually light coloured \noutcrop in the region, apparently surfaces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with iron-rich sedimentary rock that washed in \nfrom surrounding regions billions of years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s where Perseverance stumbled on \nthis spot. A collection of millimeter-scale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369120,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spots and rings that have been dubbed poppy \nseeds and leopard spots. The patterns sparked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "immediate interest because they look for \nall the world just like deposits made by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth microbes—metabolic rings \nthat expand as a colony grows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, Perseverance began its work. It drilled \nsamples for Raman spectroscopy and bombarded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ground to analyze reflectance and X-ray \nfluorescence. Then it sent the data home  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "via orbiting relay and the team on Earth who \npainstakingly analyzed the transmitted data for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many months. The team concluded that the rings \nand spots are some sort of iron phosphate,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the pale interiors of the rings were \nan iron sulphide. Most likely vivianite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surrounding greigite. The team also found a \nstrong association of organic molecules with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these patterns—and organic does not mean life, \nbut rather the carbon-based molecules that life  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428560,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tends to use. This all suggested a particular \nstory - oxidation of organic molecules leading  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to reduction of iron in the sediment into iron \nphosphates first, and then into sulphides."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442400,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is wild because it’s exactly what certain \nEarth microbes do. They extract energy via redox  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reactions of minerals, grow and spread in the \nprocess, often leading to similar structures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alternative explanations do exist—the same \nreactions can happen in high heat or acidic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462880,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "environments, however this once warm, wet river \ndelta shows no signs of either. Nor of potential  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geothermal sulphur sources that would be \nthe other explanation for the greigite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This all sounds pretty exciting. But it \nalso sounds familiar. We’ve seen these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480480,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "headlines before—scientists \nfind biomarkers for life,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484240,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only to find abiotic—non-living—processes that \ncan cause the same thing. Or, at best, for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potential biomarker to hang as a mere potential \nwithout new evidence to confirm or refute."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497600,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s take a look at some \nof the previous \"maybe aliens\"  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501440,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see if there’s a difference to this one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A little over a year ago, NASA reported \nthe tentative JWST detection of dimethyl  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sulphide in the atmosphere of a planet \norbiting a red dwarf 124 light years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514720,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away. This molecule is a major bioproduct \nof microbial metabolism on Earth. There’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no known abiotic mechanism to produce it at \nleast in the very high quantities needed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527760,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explain this observation. However, further \nobservations have not made that detection  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535520,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any more confident—it remains very \ntentative despite further effort."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a similar vein, in 2020, a team of astronomers \nreported the detection of phosphine in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546320,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere of Venus. Again, phosphine is a common \nmicrobial byproduct, and so the team claimed this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a potential biosignature. The Venutian \nsurface is pretty uninhabitable, so the idea  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here is floating colonies at altitudes closer \nto Earth conditions. And then there are these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shifting dark patterns in the venutian atmosphere, \nwhich Carl Sagan proposed could be microorganisms  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571360,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back in 1963, and which seemed to show unusual \nUV absorption characteristics in a 2019 study."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579280,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So life on venus!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The problem is, neither the phosphine \ndetection has not been confirmed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and there are potential abiotic mechanisms for \nboth the phosphine and for the dark streaks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591200,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are plenty more would-be \nbiosignatures, but let’s go back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Mars—or at least to a chunk of it that \nended up as a meteorite on Earth after,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "presumably, being ejected from its home \nplanet by another impact. Scientists found  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a peculiarly cylindrical mineral formation \nin this meteorite that they claimed might be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a fossil microbe. Chemical analysis is tricky \nin this case because of Earthly contamination,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the speculation is all around the shape of the \nthing. But then, being the kill-joys they’re paid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be, other scientists pointed out abiotic \nmeans of getting the same shape structures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, the common thread here is that previous \n“potential biosignature” discoveries either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have clear abiotic explanations or have not \nyet been verified as definite discoveries,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641280,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or both. The case of the Bright Angel find \nfeels different. There really isn’t yet an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obvious abiotic alternative—at least \nnot yet. Some new geochemistry could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certainly be at work, or maybe some \nfreak circumstances veiled the hot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659120,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or acidic, or sulphurous history of this region, \nconfusing our story. But previous biosignature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "candidates have all been quite a bit more \ntentative or more abiotically explainable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point, it’s not possible to \nassess the likelihood of this being real.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only thing that’s possible right \nnow is a kind of gut check. On one hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "almost every potential discovery that promises \nto revolutionize our entire understanding of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe turns out to be wrong. These \nsorts of false positives are expected.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693120,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this advanced stage in the development of \nscience, truly revolutionary discoveries are very,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701520,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very rare, and false positives only have to be \nslightly less rare to show up more often than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real thing. It’s a combination of this reasoning \nand a sort of cultivated skepticism that leads  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "me to expect that some brilliant geochemist \nwill find a boringly abiotic explanation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719600,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the one hand, the NASA experts who spent a \nyear analyzing the evidence do seem … unusually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "excited. And as maybe another data point, \nwe’re doing an episode on it when we don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cover every “potential biosignature.” \nThis one seems special. Special enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it’s got me and others thinking about \nthe implications. So let’s talk about that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Currently, we know of one place in the universe \nwhere life appeared—Earth. And we know that life  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appeared quickly, which makes it tempting to \nthink that life emerges easily given the right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conditions. Even if “right conditions” means \nonly very Earth-like planets, there are many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billions of such planets in our galaxy alone. This \nimplies that life is very common in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768400,
    "dDurationMs": 9520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or … not. There are strong arguments for why the \nearly emergence of Earth life tells us very little  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777920,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the broader abundance of life. They come \ndown to selection bias and anthropic reasoning.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not surprising that we observe our home \nplanet as having life because the alternative is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible. But that only puts us on a planet with \nlife, not on one where life formed early—which,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796800,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again, we wouldn’t expect if life is improbable. \nBut there’s another factor to consider. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formation of life might be extremely sensitive to \nthe conditions of the planet. In some places it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty improbable, in others much more \nprobable. The latter may be quite rare,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the Earth. Depending on how this \nbiogenesis probability depends on conditions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819440,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it might be that only near-perfectly tuned \nplanets get life, and they get it early."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the argument made, for example, by \nDavid Speigel and Ed Turner, they show that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the early emergence of life on Earth does \nnot guarantee abundant extraterrestrial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life and that's the case if the prior \nprobability of that abiogenesis is very "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845680,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sensitive to initial conditions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "David Kipping adds to the analysis \nthe fact that significant time is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853760,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needed for life to spawn a sentient species,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that this timespan starts to bump against \nthe maximum habitability of Earth-like planets.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864080,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He finds that the early emergence of life on Earth \nis perfectly consistent with life being rare."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all of this changes if we find one \nmore incidence of life in the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it changes massively if we find it on Mars. \nThat’s a huge hit to the hypothesis that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "initial formation of life is improbable, \nand to the idea its probability is super  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sensitive to the conditions. If life formed on \nMars then life is not overly picky about where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894800,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it forms. And if it did so quickly on Mars \nthen that short timescale is not biased by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the need of lots of time to then form us, as was \nthe case for Earth. The Speigel and Turner paper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also quantifies this in light of the discovery \nof early life on Mars. In short, the discovery  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of ancient life on Mars short-circuits the \nanthropic arguments for life being very rare."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, intelligent and technological life \nmay still be very rare, and our non-observation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928960,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of advanced species arising before us on the \ngalactic scale IS evidence of that rarity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This leads to an awkward conclusion. \nIf primitive life is extremely common,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943120,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if it almost never reaches a stage \ncapable of being seen across the stars,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then something impedes that development \nafter the abiogenesis stage."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This idea is often framed as the \n“great filter”--one or more steps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the development of civilizations that are \nso ridiculously hard to cross that very few  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potential starting points actually make it all \nthe way to star-hopping sci-fi mega-civilization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971440,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the question has always been, is humanity one \nof the lucky few that has passed its great filter?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978800,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or is that filter still ahead of us? Was that \nhard transition abiogenesis, or the development  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of complex cells or big brains, or all of these \ntogether? Or is it the survival from digital age  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992800,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to space age that’s so damn tricky, due, perhaps, \nto species almost invariably wiping themselves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000400,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out. We don’t know, but the more we rule out \ngreat filters in the past, the more likely it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that the great filter is in the future. Nick \nBostrom and others have articulated this argument."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Currently, the probability of abiogenesis on \na given suitable planet could be as small as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one in many, many billions—because anthropic \nselection allows such a rare phenomenon to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026960,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observed by the observers it produces. Even if \nthat’s one lifeform per galaxy or universe. Or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033360,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the probability of abiogenesis could be closer to \none—100%--which means many billions of incidents  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040560,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of abiogenesis in this galaxy alone. But the \nfilter has to be there somewhere. We don't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see aliens. If we eliminate early filters, \nthen later filters have to pick up the slack.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Abiogenesis could do a lot of work in pushing \nthe filter into our past, but an observation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of life on Mars reduces the filtering effort of \nabiogenesis by a factor of up to many billions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065280,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we have found life on Mars, at the very \nleast we should stop taking for granted that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll survive into the future, because there’s now \neven stronger evidence of our existential risk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should add that there’s another story \nthat allows us to keep feeling special— and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083760,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's panspermia. It could also be that life \nformed only once and early on either Earth or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090800,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mars and then early microbes hitched rides on \nimpact ejecta to travel between the planets.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then we’re back to wondering if we’re alone, \nand we also get to wonder if we’re Martians."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But putting existential anguish and \nplanetary identity issues aside, let’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allow ourselves to be excited at the potential \ndiscovery of our extinct planetary neighbors,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114240,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least for a bit. Did we find life on \nMars? We can absolutely know for sure "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121040,
    "dDurationMs": 9360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether the Bright Angel patterns were biological \nwhen we can collect those samples. Perseverance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130400,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "packaged the samples and leaves them waiting for a \nupcoming mission designed to return them to Earth.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That mission was cancelled in the last White \nHouse budget, so, there’s that. There was some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "verbal backtracking after the NASA announcement \nbut the retrieval of those samples is pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149840,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unclear. It seems to me that in these times of \ndivision, our first discovery of life on another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1156720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world could be just what we need to reignite \nsome collective pride. And maybe to get on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1162560,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with our real job of cooperatively exploring and \nmaybe even contacting a life-filled space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1170640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Boot.Dev for supporting PBS. Boot.dev \nknows that learning programming is challenging,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176800,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but they believe that it doesn’t have to be \nthe kind of challenge that makes you smash  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1180080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your keyboard and give up. Boot.dev uses tactics \nfrom modern game design to help students learn  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1185840,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coding and build real projects in Python, SQL and \nGo. On boot.dev you’ll be trained by a personal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1192960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI tutor named Boots (a wizard bear)** who will \nguide you as you earn XP, unlock Achievements,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1199600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complete quests and fight bosses that help you \nlearn to code. All users can read and watch all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1205920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "content, but Boot.dev members unlock interactive \nfeatures like hands-on coding, AI assistance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212160,
    "dDurationMs": 4684,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "progress tracking, and game mechanics. \nYou’ll also get access to the Boot.dev  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "community which has tons of real \nhumans to help if you ever get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1222080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stuck. So if you’ve ever wanted to learn \nto code, Boot.dev is here. Go to Boot.dev  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1228400,
    "dDurationMs": 17440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and use the code SPACETIME to get 25% off \nyour entire first year on the annual plan."
    } ]
  } ]
}
